# Data-Driven Testing in Playwright

## What is Data-Driven Testing?

Data-Driven Testing is a testing technique where the same test is executed multiple times using different sets of test data.

Instead of creating separate test cases for each user or scenario, we keep the test logic the same and only change the input data.

--------------------------------------------------

## Why Do We Use Data-Driven Testing?

Benefits:

- Reduces duplicate code
- Improves maintainability
- Increases reusability
- Easy to add new test data
- Supports multiple test scenarios

--------------------------------------------------

## Without Data-Driven Testing

test('Login with standard user', async ({ page }) => {

});

test('Login with problem user', async ({ page }) => {

});

test('Login with error user', async ({ page }) => {

});

Problem:

- Same login steps repeated
- More code
- Difficult maintenance

--------------------------------------------------

## With Data-Driven Testing

Create test data:

const users = [

{
    username: 'standard_user',
    password: 'secret_sauce'
},

{
    username: 'problem_user',
    password: 'secret_sauce'
},

{
    username: 'error_user',
    password: 'secret_sauce'
}

];

Use a loop:

for(const user of users) {

    test(`Login with ${user.username}`, async ({ page }) => {

        // Login steps

    });

}

--------------------------------------------------

## Complete Example

import { test, expect } from '@playwright/test';

const users = [

{
    username: 'standard_user',
    password: 'secret_sauce'
},

{
    username: 'problem_user',
    password: 'secret_sauce'
},

{
    username: 'error_user',
    password: 'secret_sauce'
}

];

for(const user of users) {

    test(`Login with ${user.username}`, async ({ page }) => {

        await page.goto('https://www.saucedemo.com/');

        await page.locator('#user-name')
            .fill(user.username);

        await page.locator('#password')
            .fill(user.password);

        await page.locator('#login-button')
            .click();

        await expect(
            page.locator('[data-test="title"]')
        ).toHaveText('Products');

    });

}

--------------------------------------------------

## How It Works

Data:

const users = [

{
    username: 'standard_user',
    password: 'secret_sauce'
},

{
    username: 'problem_user',
    password: 'secret_sauce'
}

];

Loop:

for(const user of users)

Iteration 1:

user.username = standard_user

Creates test:

Login with standard_user

--------------------------------------------------

Iteration 2:

user.username = problem_user

Creates test:

Login with problem_user

--------------------------------------------------

Result:

Playwright creates separate tests automatically using the same logic but different data.

--------------------------------------------------

## Why Array of Objects?

Because each user can have multiple properties.

Example:

{
    username: 'standard_user',
    password: 'secret_sauce'
}

Object can store:

- username
- password
- role
- expected result
- error message

